<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="height: 50px;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="height: 50px;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Role in the World</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Importance of Panama in Global Trade</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="height: 50px;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Evaluate texts for bias. Reading: Understand the relationship between main points and examples in various texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can evaluate bias in texts about the Canal expansion, identifying whether the text emphasizes economic gains but downplays the impact on biodiversity (e.g., "The text mentions job creation, but omits the effects on mangrove ecosystems.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can understand relationships between trade benefits and environmental concerns in Canal texts (e.g., "While the Canal boosts trade, it also affects local ecosystems.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can identify examples that support or contradict the argument that the Canal's expansion has increased national revenue at the expense of water conservation (e.g., "Some reports praise trade efficiency, while others warn about water shortages in Lake Gatún.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images related to the Panama Canal (ships, locks, cargo, rainforest, diverse ecosystems, construction workers). Students, in pairs, brainstorm and list 5-7 words they remember from the previous lesson related to these images. Then, they share their lists with the class. This activates previously learned vocabulary such as 'Panama Canal', 'shipping', 'trade', 'traffic', 'congestion', 'investments', 'environmental groups', 'ecosystems'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the concept of evaluating texts for bias. "Today, we're going to become detectives of information. We'll read texts and try to figure out if the author is showing a preference or leaning towards one side more than another." The teacher models skimming a short paragraph (e.g., a short news headline) and identifying words that might indicate a positive or negative slant. The teacher specifically models the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "environmental" /ɪnˌvaɪ.rənˈmen.təl/ and "impactful" /ɪmˈpækt.fəl/, and the long vowel sound /oʊ/ in words like "global" and "local."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a text only talks about the good things about the Canal, but doesn't mention any challenges, is it biased?" (Expected answer: Yes.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important for us to be able to identify bias when we read about important topics like the Panama Canal's role in global trade?" (Expected answer: To get a complete and fair understanding, to form our own informed opinions.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short article (approx. 250-300 words) adapted from a news report or an opinion piece, titled "Navigating the Future: Panama Canal's Dual Impact on Global Trade and Local Ecosystems." The text discusses the Canal's economic benefits (e.g., 'efficiency', 'investments', 'global relationships', 'revenue') and potential environmental concerns (e.g., 'ecosystems', 'water conservation', 'affect', 'raise concerns'). The text incorporates passive voice, present perfect continuous, and elements that could lead to discussions of bias.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student evaluates texts for bias and distinguishes between main points and supporting examples, understanding complex information about global issues like trade and environmental impact.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly and answer: "What is the main topic of this article? Does it present only one side of the Canal's impact or both?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False/Not Mentioned:</b> Students read specific statements and decide if they are True, False, or Not Mentioned in the text. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The Canal has been improving global trade for decades."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Environmental groups have been raising concerns about water conservation."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The recent expansion completed ahead of schedule."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify Main Points:</b> Students identify the main points related to economic benefits and main points related to environmental concerns.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Evaluating bias in texts, understanding relationship between main points and examples. Target grammar: Passive voice, Second and third conditionals, Present Perfect Continuous. Target vocabulary: 'operation', 'traffic', 'congestion', 'efficiency', 'costs', 'delays', 'investments', 'environmental groups', 'ecosystems', 'authority', 'global relationships', 'influence', 'construction', 'future', 'operate', 'manage', 'monitor', 'prevent', 'invest', 'enhance', 'raise concerns', 'affect', 'impact', 'influence', 'global', 'expensive', 'efficient', 'long-term', 'environmental', 'impactful', 'sustainable', 'monitored', 'influential', 'significant', 'costly', 'congested', 'speculative'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Grammar Spotting:</b> Students re-read the article and identify all sentences written in the passive voice (e.g., "The Canal has been managed by the Panama Canal Authority since 1999."), and sentences using the Present Perfect Continuous (e.g., "The Canal has been improving global trade, but it has also been causing environmental concerns."). They underline these structures and identify the subjects and agents where applicable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Identifying Main Points and Examples:</b> In small groups, students re-read paragraphs specifically designed to show potential bias. For each paragraph, they must:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify the main argument presented.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Underline specific examples provided to support that argument.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discuss whether the text gives equal weight to both positive and negative aspects. For instance, "The text mentions job creation, but it omits the effects on mangrove ecosystems, so it *is being* somewhat biased."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Hypothetical Scenarios:</b> Students work in pairs to discuss hypothetical situations related to the Canal's impact, using Second and Third Conditionals. They consider how the information presented in the article might change their perspectives.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If Panama had not built the Canal, global trade would have faced significant challenges in the early 20th century. How would the global economy be different now?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If the government invested more in sustainable water management, what positive impacts could be observed in the local ecosystems?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If environmental groups hadn't raised concerns, what might have been the long-term impact on the Canal's surrounding areas?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Presentation and Justification.</b> Students form groups of 3-4. Each group receives a different short text (or a different section/perspective from a longer text) about the Panama Canal's impact (e.g., one focusing on economic gains, another on environmental challenges, a third on technological advancements). Their task is to:
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Read their assigned text carefully, identifying the main points and supporting examples.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evaluate their text for bias: Does it seem to favor one perspective? What information might be downplayed or omitted? (e.g., "The text emphasizes economic gains but downplays the impact on biodiversity.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prepare a short presentation (2-3 minutes) to the class summarizing their text's main arguments, the examples used, and their evaluation of its bias. They must use specific vocabulary like 'efficiency', 'costs', 'investments', 'ecosystems', 'influence', and integrate target grammar (passive voice, conditionals, present perfect continuous) where appropriate. For example, "While the Canal boosts trade, it also affects local ecosystems. If more sustainable practices had been implemented earlier, perhaps the environmental concerns wouldn't be as significant today." The pronunciation of /oʊ/ in words like "global" and "show" should be clear.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly links to "The Importance of Panama in Global Trade" and "Panama's Role in the World" by requiring students to critically analyze and articulate various perspectives on the Canal's multifaceted impact, mirroring real-world discussions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback.</b> The teacher uses a checklist to observe each group's presentation, focusing on their ability to identify main points and examples, evaluate bias, and use target language. Peers provide constructive feedback on clarity and supporting evidence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student submits a short written reflection (3-5 sentences) summarizing their group's findings about bias in their assigned text and how they identified it. They should include one example from their text to support their claim about bias, and aim to incorporate at least one target grammatical structure (passive voice, conditional, or present perfect continuous).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Clearly identifies the main argument and examples from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Bias Evaluation:</b> Successfully identifies potential bias and justifies it with evidence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Employs at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., 'efficiency', 'ecosystems', 'influence', 'raise concerns').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Use:</b> Incorporates at least one of the target grammatical structures (passive voice, conditionals, present perfect continuous) correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation (during presentation):</b> Intelligible pronunciation, especially of challenging sounds like /oʊ/ and multisyllabic words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of identifying bias in the text today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now about understanding the relationship between a text's main points and its supporting examples?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies did I use today that helped me understand the texts better, and how can I apply them to other subjects?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has my understanding of the Panama Canal's role, specifically its dual impact on trade and environment, changed after evaluating texts for bias?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of identifying bias and understanding text structure worked well? Did students effectively use the target grammar and vocabulary to discuss their findings? Were there any challenges in evaluating bias, and what adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for similar reading tasks in the future? Specifically, how well did students grasp the relationship between 'global relationships' and 'influence', or 'investments' and 'future' in the context of the Canal?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will engage in a structured debate or discussion about the different perspectives on the Panama Canal's impact, drawing upon the critical reading skills they developed today to articulate informed arguments and respectfully discuss varying viewpoints, using the target vocabulary and grammatical structures in spoken discourse.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>